#!/bin/sh
# script/mutation-census: the per-crate mutation record, and the diff between two censuses
# See notes/mutation-testing.md for what a mutant and a survivor mean, notes/mutation-census.md for
# this record's own page, and notes/project-metrics.md for the other committed series.
# Milestone 518 (a census that cannot be attributed is a number nobody can act on).
#
# **A census that cannot be attributed is a number nobody can act on.** `script/mutation --report`
# prints a per-crate table from a finished run and then the run output is thrown away, so the tree
# had kept exactly one per-crate record in its life: `.cargo/mutants-baseline.txt`, then dated
# 2026-08-03. On 2026-09-19 `design/fatal-risks.md`'s third risk recorded that the score had fallen
# between two whole-corpus censuses five days apart and had to say in the same breath that **which
# crates caused it is unknown**, because 2026-09-14's per-crate numbers were never written down.
# This script is that gap closed: one row per crate per census, committed, plus a `--compare` that
# says which crates moved and by how much.
#
#   script/mutation-census --add-run 35421192143      capture a finished GitHub Actions census
#   script/mutation-census --add 2026-09-19 35421192143 DIR...   capture local run output
#   script/mutation-census --list                     the censuses on record
#   script/mutation-census --show 2026-09-19          one census, per crate
#   script/mutation-census --compare 2026-09-14 2026-09-19    which crates moved
#   script/mutation-census --renames                  regenerate the rename table from git
#
# The record is `notes/project-metrics/mutation-census.csv`, one row per crate per census:
#
#     census,run,crate,tested,caught,missed,timeout,unviable,real_holes
#
# `tested` is the other four added up, so a truncated or double-counted row fails its own
# arithmetic rather than looking plausible. `crate` is **the name the run recorded**, never
# rewritten, for the reason the blind-`sed` scar in AGENTS.md gives: a sweep that renames history to
# match the present destroys the evidence that the present is different. Resolution happens at
# comparison time, out of the rename table below.
#
# **`real_holes` is the count the mutation measurement exists for**: the survivors no algebraic
# argument answers, per helpers/mutation_real_holes.py's classes (a `gap` row, a `killed` row the
# census contradicts, no row, an `equivalent` row that rests on today's callers or on this harness).
# `--add` computes it from the census's own shard output against the triage file of the recording
# day, so the column is a point-in-time reading that never moves; the live reading, regenerated per
# census, is notes/mutation-testing/survivor-classification.md. A blank cell means the census was
# recorded before the column existed; the classification page is the only source for those.
#
# **`killed` counts a timeout as a kill and excludes unviable mutants from the denominator**, which
# is notes/mutation-testing.md's definition ("`killed%` counts a timeout as a kill, because every
# one of the 96 was checked by hand and every one is a detected hang", and "an unviable mutant does
# not compile, which says nothing about the tests"). It is stated here rather than assumed because
# the one time two censuses were compared by hand, the two rows were computed under two different
# definitions and the comparison inverted; see BUGS.
#
# **Why a rename table, and why it is derived rather than remembered.** Between the 2026-09-14 and
# 2026-09-19 censuses, milestone 265 (`_proto` is a truncation)'s sweep renamed sixteen crates (`ntp_proto` to
# `network_time_protocol` and its siblings), and three more followed on 2026-09-18 (`dtb`, `ipc`,
# `gpt`). A join on the bare crate name silently drops every renamed crate from a like-for-like
# comparison, which is not a smaller answer but a wrong one: the crates that vanish take their
# mutants with them and the remaining rate is over a different corpus. `--renames` derives the table
# from `git log --diff-filter=R` over `crates/*/src/lib.rs` and writes
# `notes/project-metrics/mutation-census-renames.csv`.
#
# **It follows `src/lib.rs` and not `Cargo.toml`, and that is a scar.** Git pairs renames by content
# similarity, and a commit that renames several crates at once gives it a pile of four-line
# `Cargo.toml` files that are nearly identical to each other. Asked about `Cargo.toml` it returns
# confident nonsense: `crates/uheap -> crates/line_editor` and `crates/canary_gate ->
# crates/work_steal_slot`, cross-matched pairs from one sweep. A crate's `lib.rs` is distinctive, so
# the same query over it returns `canary_gate -> memory_corruption_canary_gate` and
# `steal_request -> work_steal_slot`, which is what actually happened. The table is committed rather
# than derived on every run so that a wrong pairing can be corrected by hand and stays corrected.
#
# EXAMPLES
#
# Capture the weekly census as soon as the workflow goes green, which is the habit this milestone
# exists to establish (the artifacts expire; the record does not):
#
#     $ script/mutation-census --add-run 35421192143
#     ==> downloading run 35421192143 (2026-09-19)
#     ==> 62 crates, 9,656 mutants, 8,925 viable, 93.7% killed
#     ==> wrote 62 rows to notes/project-metrics/mutation-census.csv
#
# Ask the question nobody could answer on 2026-09-19:
#
#     $ script/mutation-census --compare 2026-09-14 2026-09-19
#     62 crates in both, 2 only in 2026-09-14, 0 only in 2026-09-19
#     killed 92.3% -> 93.7% over the crates in both (+1.42 points)
#     survivors 687 -> 563, viable 8,893 -> 8,925
#
#     crate                        viable          killed        survivors   points
#     timetable               182 ->   146   73.6% -> 100.0%   48 ->    0      0.51
#     filesystem_contract     598 ->   598   90.1% ->  93.3%   59 ->   40      0.21
#     machine_discovery       615 ->   622   88.1% ->  87.6%   73 ->   77     -0.04
#     multicast_dns_protocol  362 ->     -   77.3% ->      -   82 ->    -   (gone)
#     ...
#
# `points` is that crate's exact contribution to the corpus-level move, and the column sums to it:
# `((killed_b - killed_a) - rate_a * (viable_b - viable_a)) / viable_b`. A crate can move the rate
# without its own rate moving, by growing or shrinking against the corpus average, and the
# decomposition says so instead of leaving a reader to guess.
#
# Move the fixed point every `script/mutation --report` diffs against to a recorded whole census, once
# its survivors are triaged (a baseline written before then makes the untriaged ones the norm):
#
#     $ script/mutation-census --save-baseline 2026-10-19
#     ==> wrote 101 crates from the 2026-10-19 census to .cargo/mutants-baseline.txt
#
# Name: provisional, coined 2026-09-20 by milestone 518's lane. `census` is the word
# `design/fatal-risks.md` and `notes/mutation-testing.md` already use for a whole-corpus run ("it is
# a census rather than a sample"), so this names the function in the tree's own vocabulary, and the
# `mutation-` prefix is what says which census: this tree also takes an `unsafe` census and a thread
# census. `mutation-history` and `mutation-trend` were both refused for saying what the file is used
# for rather than what it holds; a census is the thing recorded, and a trend is one question asked of
# several. Folding it into `script/mutation` as `--census` was refused for a different reason: that
# script installs a pinned tool and runs it for hours, and this one reads a CSV. The two CSV files
# and `notes/mutation-census.md` carry the same provisional name and stand or fall with it.
#
# BUGS
#
# - **The record starts on 2026-08-03 and has a four-census hole before it that nothing can fill.**
#   Only three whole-corpus censuses have ever succeeded (2026-09-14, 2026-09-16, 2026-09-19) plus
#   the August baseline, and they are all in. The scheduled runs from 2026-08-10 to 2026-08-31 are
#   in the record's range and are absent because they produced no result at all; `mutation.yml`'s
#   own `BUGS` section has why. The 2026-09-03 run produced one shard of eight, which is a sample
#   and not a census, and is deliberately not a row here: a sample cannot tell an absent mutant from
#   a killed one, so a per-crate row from one would read as a crate that got worse.
# - **The 2026-08-03 row's counts are derived, not observed**, and the file it came from said so: the
#   2026-08-03 run was resumed twice, so `caught` there is total-minus-the-rest and three of 5,551 mutants could
#   not be matched to any pass. It is ingested as written. A comparison against it is a comparison
#   against a reconstruction.
# - **The 2026-08-03 row's crate names are half-swept.** Its source file had been rewritten by the
#   rename sweeps, so the row spells `device_tree_blob` for a run that recorded `dtb` but still
#   spells `cred` for `credentialer`. The rename table resolves both, so a comparison is right.
# - **`--save-baseline` trusts the record to hold only whole censuses.** mutation.yml's record job
#   refuses a partial one, but `--add` and `--add-run` do not check, so a census added by hand from
#   a lost shard would pass. Its crate check catches a per-crate subset, not a missing shard.
# - **Nothing gates capture.** A census can still be run and thrown away, exactly as four of them
#   were; this only makes it one command not to. A gate would have to know a run happened, and the
#   thing that knows is the workflow, which does not write to the tree.
# - **A crate deleted and a crate renamed to something git could not follow look identical here**,
#   and both print as "only in A". `multicast_dns_protocol` and `multicast_dns_config` are that
#   case between 2026-09-14 and 2026-09-19.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

exec python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'
import collections
import csv
import io
import json
import os
import pathlib
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import typing

# helpers/mutation_real_holes.py classes the survivors and counts the real holes; the import keeps
# to helpers like script/metrics's, and `dont_write_bytecode` (script/lint's python) keeps a
# __pycache__ out of a dirty worktree.
sys.path.insert(0, "helpers")
import mutation_real_holes

RECORD = pathlib.Path("notes/project-metrics/mutation-census.csv")
RENAMES = pathlib.Path("notes/project-metrics/mutation-census-renames.csv")
FIELDS = ["census", "run", "crate", "tested", "caught", "missed", "timeout", "unviable", "real_holes"]
KINDS = ["caught", "missed", "timeout", "unviable"]


class Row(typing.NamedTuple):
    """One recorded crate: the run that measured it, its four counts, and its real holes (a blank
    string for a census recorded before the column existed)."""

    run: str
    counts: collections.Counter
    real_holes: str


def usage():
    print(
        "usage: script/mutation-census --list | --show CENSUS | --compare A B\n"
        "                             | --add DATE RUN DIR... | --add-run RUN\n"
        "                             | --add-baseline | --check-baseline | --save-baseline CENSUS\n"
        "                             | --renames",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    return 2


def load_renames():
    """old -> new, one hop. Missing file is not an error: an empty table compares raw names."""
    chain = {}
    if RENAMES.exists():
        for row in csv.DictReader(RENAMES.open()):
            chain[row["old"]] = row["new"]
    return chain


def resolve(name, chain):
    """Follow the rename chain to today's name, stopping on a cycle rather than spinning."""
    seen = set()
    while name in chain and name not in seen:
        seen.add(name)
        name = chain[name]
    return name


def load_record():
    """census -> crate -> Row. Raw crate names, as recorded. A record written before the
    `real_holes` column reads with blank cells, and the next write brings it to the new shape."""
    out = collections.defaultdict(dict)
    if not RECORD.exists():
        return out
    for row in csv.DictReader(RECORD.open()):
        counts = {k: int(row[k]) for k in KINDS}
        if sum(counts.values()) != int(row["tested"]):
            print(
                f"mutation-census: {RECORD}: {row['census']} {row['crate']} does not add up "
                f"(tested={row['tested']}, parts sum to {sum(counts.values())})",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            sys.exit(1)
        out[row["census"]][row["crate"]] = Row(row["run"], collections.Counter(counts),
                                               row.get("real_holes") or "")
    return out


def write_record(record):
    RECORD.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with RECORD.open("w", newline="") as fh:
        writer = csv.writer(fh)
        writer.writerow(FIELDS)
        for census in sorted(record):
            for crate in sorted(record[census]):
                row = record[census][crate]
                writer.writerow(
                    [census, row.run, crate, sum(row.counts[k] for k in KINDS)]
                    + [row.counts[k] for k in KINDS] + [row.real_holes]
                )


def viable(c):
    return c["caught"] + c["missed"] + c["timeout"]


def killed(c):
    """Caught plus timeout over viable, as notes/mutation-testing.md defines it."""
    v = viable(c)
    return 100.0 * (c["caught"] + c["timeout"]) / v if v else 0.0


def total(crates):
    t = collections.Counter()
    for c in crates:
        t.update(c)
    return t


def counts_from_dirs(dirs):
    """Per-crate counts from cargo-mutants run output, the same derivation script/mutation uses:
    each outcome file holds one mutant per line beginning with its source path, and the crate is the
    path component before /src/."""
    crates = collections.defaultdict(collections.Counter)
    seen_any = False
    for d in dirs:
        for kind in KINDS:
            path = pathlib.Path(d) / (kind + ".txt")
            if not path.exists():
                continue
            seen_any = True
            for line in path.read_text().splitlines():
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                head = line.split(":")[0]
                crates[head.split("/src/")[0].split("/")[-1]][kind] += 1
    if not seen_any:
        print(f"mutation-census: no outcome files under {' '.join(map(str, dirs))}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
    return crates


def add(census, run, crates, holes=None):
    """Record one census. `holes` is crate -> real-hole count from the census's own shard output;
    None for a census recorded without one (the baseline), which leaves the cell blank."""
    record = load_record()
    record[census] = {
        name: Row(run, c, "" if holes is None else str(holes.get(name, 0)))
        for name, c in crates.items()
    }
    t = total(crates.values())
    print(
        f"==> {len(crates)} crates, {sum(t[k] for k in KINDS):,} mutants, "
        f"{viable(t):,} viable, {killed(t):.1f}% killed, "
        f"{sum(int(h) for h in (holes or {}).values()):,} real holes"
    )
    write_record(record)
    print(f"==> wrote {len(crates)} rows to {RECORD}")
    return 0


def add_run(run_id):
    """Ingest a GitHub Actions census by its run id. The artifacts expire (90 days by default) and
    the record does not, which is the whole argument for doing this the day the run goes green."""
    if not shutil.which("gh"):
        print("mutation-census: --add-run needs the gh CLI; use --add with downloaded dirs", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    meta = subprocess.run(
        ["gh", "run", "view", run_id, "--json", "createdAt,conclusion,workflowName"],
        capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    if meta.returncode != 0:
        print(f"mutation-census: gh run view {run_id}: {meta.stderr.strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    info = json.loads(meta.stdout)
    census = info["createdAt"][:10]
    if info["conclusion"] != "success":
        # A red run is a partial corpus, and a partial corpus read per crate is worse than no row:
        # a shard that died takes whole crates' mutants with it and the survivors left behind read
        # as a regression. mutation.yml's report job says the same thing about its own summary.
        print(
            f"mutation-census: run {run_id} concluded {info['conclusion']}, not success. A partial "
            "census is a sample; see this script's BUGS. Ingest it with --add if you mean to.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 2
    print(f"==> downloading run {run_id} ({census})")
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        dl = subprocess.run(["gh", "run", "download", run_id, "-D", tmp], capture_output=True, text=True)
        if dl.returncode != 0:
            print(f"mutation-census: gh run download {run_id}: {dl.stderr.strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        # download-artifact unpacks several artifacts into subdirectories and a lone one flat, the
        # trap mutation.yml's report job records; find the run directories by what is in them.
        dirs = sorted({p.parent for p in pathlib.Path(tmp).rglob("caught.txt")})
        if not dirs:
            print(f"mutation-census: run {run_id} has no surviving outcome artifacts", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        print(f"==> {len(dirs)} shard artifacts")
        return add(census, run_id, counts_from_dirs(dirs),
                   mutation_real_holes.real_holes_from_shards(dirs))


BASELINE = pathlib.Path(".cargo/mutants-baseline.txt")
# The second line of BASELINE names the census it was written from. Nothing else says which run the
# file is: before this line existed the reader assumed a date, and that assumption outlived the file.
BASELINE_ORIGIN = re.compile(r"^# census=(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}) run=(\S+)$")


def read_baseline(require_origin=True):
    """(census, run, crate -> Counter) from BASELINE. A file whose header does not name its census is
    refused rather than dated by guess, except to --save-baseline, which only needs its crates."""
    lines = BASELINE.read_text().splitlines()
    origin = next((m for m in map(BASELINE_ORIGIN.match, lines) if m), None)
    if origin is None and require_origin:
        print(f"mutation-census: {BASELINE} has no '# census=DATE run=RUN' line, so nothing says "
              "which census it is; rewrite it with --save-baseline CENSUS", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(2)
    crates = {}
    for line in lines:
        if line.startswith("#") or not line.strip():
            continue
        name, caught, missed, timeout, unviable = line.split()
        crates[name] = collections.Counter(
            caught=int(caught), missed=int(missed), timeout=int(timeout), unviable=int(unviable)
        )
    return (origin.group(1), origin.group(2), crates) if origin else (None, None, crates)


def add_baseline(check=False):
    """Ingest BASELINE as the census its header names, or confirm the record already holds it. With
    `check` (script/lint), a census the record lacks is a failure rather than something to add."""
    census, run, crates = read_baseline()
    record = load_record()
    if check and census not in record:
        print(f"mutation-census: {BASELINE} names the {census} census, which {RECORD} lacks", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if census in record:
        held = {name: row.counts for name, row in record[census].items()}
        if held != crates:
            print(f"mutation-census: {BASELINE} disagrees with the record's {census} rows", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print(f"==> {BASELINE} is the recorded {census} census, row for row; nothing to add")
        return 0
    return add(census, run, crates)


def save_baseline(census):
    """Rewrite BASELINE from a census already in the record, never from run directories.

    The record only holds whole censuses (mutation.yml's record job refuses a partial one), so this
    cannot be handed eight per-crate runs and replace the corpus with eight rows. As a second check,
    every crate the current baseline holds must be in the census unless its package is gone."""
    record = load_record()
    if census not in record:
        print(f"mutation-census: no {census} census in {RECORD}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    rows = record[census]
    chain = load_renames()
    have = {resolve(name, chain) for name in rows}
    if BASELINE.exists():
        _, _, current = read_baseline(require_origin=False)
        lost = sorted(
            name for name in current
            if resolve(name, chain) not in have
            and any(pathlib.Path(d, resolve(name, chain), "src").is_dir() for d in (".", "crates"))
        )
        if lost:
            print(f"mutation-census: the {census} census lacks {', '.join(lost)}, which the current "
                  "baseline holds and the tree still has; a partial census is not a baseline", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
    runs = {row.run for row in rows.values()}
    if len(runs) != 1:
        print(f"mutation-census: the {census} census has rows from {len(runs)} runs", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    with BASELINE.open("w") as fh:
        fh.write("# Written by script/mutation-census --save-baseline: crate caught missed timeout unviable.\n")
        fh.write(f"# census={census} run={runs.pop()}\n")
        fh.write("# The prose baseline, and what each survivor means, is notes/mutation-testing.md.\n")
        for name in sorted(rows):
            c = rows[name].counts
            fh.write(f"{name} {c['caught']} {c['missed']} {c['timeout']} {c['unviable']}\n")
    print(f"==> wrote {len(rows)} crates from the {census} census to {BASELINE}")
    return 0


def renames():
    """Derive the rename table from git. See the header for why this follows lib.rs."""
    out = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "log", "--name-status", "--diff-filter=R", "--find-renames",
         "--format=%H %ad", "--date=format-local:%Y-%m-%d", "--reverse", "--", "crates/*/src/lib.rs"],
        # format-local under TZ=UTC: --date=short prints the committer's own zone, and this tree's
        # dates are UTC, so a rename made in a US evening was being filed under the day before.
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=True, env={**os.environ, "TZ": "UTC"},
    ).stdout
    rows, commit, when = [], "", ""
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if line and not line.startswith("R"):
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) == 2:
                commit, when = parts[0][:8], parts[1]
            continue
        if not line.startswith("R"):
            continue
        _, old, new = line.split("\t")
        old, new = old.split("/")[1], new.split("/")[1]
        if old != new:
            rows.append((old, new, commit, when))
    RENAMES.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with RENAMES.open("w", newline="") as fh:
        writer = csv.writer(fh)
        writer.writerow(["old", "new", "commit", "date"])
        writer.writerows(rows)
    print(f"==> wrote {len(rows)} renames to {RENAMES}")
    return 0


def show(census):
    record = load_record()
    if census not in record:
        print(f"mutation-census: no census {census} on record", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    crates = record[census]
    run = next(iter(crates.values())).run
    known = sum(1 for row in crates.values() if row.real_holes != "")
    print(f"census {census} (run {run}), {len(crates)} crates"
          + ("" if known else ", recorded before real holes were measured"))
    print(f"{'crate':<44}{'caught':>7}{'missed':>8}{'timeout':>9}{'unviable':>10}{'viable':>8}"
          f"{'killed':>9}{'real holes':>12}")
    for name in sorted(crates):
        row = crates[name]
        holes = row.real_holes or "-"
        print(f"{name:<44}{row.counts['caught']:>7}{row.counts['missed']:>8}{row.counts['timeout']:>9}"
              f"{row.counts['unviable']:>10}{viable(row.counts):>8}{killed(row.counts):>8.1f}%"
              f"{holes:>12}")
    t = total(row.counts for row in crates.values())
    holes = total_holes(crates)
    print(f"{'TOTAL':<44}{t['caught']:>7}{t['missed']:>8}{t['timeout']:>9}{t['unviable']:>10}"
          f"{viable(t):>8}{killed(t):>8.1f}%{holes:>12}")
    return 0


def total_holes(crates):
    """The census's real holes summed over the rows that carry a count, or `-` when none do."""
    known = [int(row.real_holes) for row in crates.values() if row.real_holes != ""]
    return str(sum(known)) if known else "-"


def by_current_name(crates, chain):
    """Fold a census's raw crate names onto today's names, so two censuses join across renames.
    Returns (crate -> counts, crate -> real holes or None); a crate recorded before the column, or
    a rename group any of whose members lacks a count, carries None. A group with one blank member
    is not measured, never a partial sum: a sum over some members reads as the whole crate."""
    out = collections.defaultdict(collections.Counter)
    holes = collections.defaultdict(lambda: None)
    blank = set()
    for name, row in crates.items():
        resolved = resolve(name, chain)
        out[resolved].update(row.counts)
        if row.real_holes == "":
            blank.add(resolved)
        else:
            holes[resolved] = (holes[resolved] or 0) + int(row.real_holes)
    for name in blank:
        holes[name] = None
    return out, holes


def compare(a, b):
    record = load_record()
    for census in (a, b):
        if census not in record:
            print(f"mutation-census: no census {census} on record", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    chain = load_renames()
    ca, ha = by_current_name(record[a], chain)
    cb, hb = by_current_name(record[b], chain)
    both = sorted(set(ca) & set(cb))
    only_a = sorted(set(ca) - set(cb))
    only_b = sorted(set(cb) - set(ca))

    ta, tb = total(ca[k] for k in both), total(cb[k] for k in both)
    rate_a, rate_b = killed(ta), killed(tb)
    holes_a = sum(ha[k] for k in both if ha[k] is not None and hb[k] is not None) \
        if all(ha[k] is not None and hb[k] is not None for k in both) else None
    holes_b = sum(hb[k] for k in both if ha[k] is not None and hb[k] is not None) \
        if holes_a is not None else None
    print(f"{len(both)} crates in both, {len(only_a)} only in {a}, {len(only_b)} only in {b}")
    print(f"killed {rate_a:.1f}% -> {rate_b:.1f}% over the crates in both ({rate_b - rate_a:+.2f} points)")
    print(f"survivors {ta['missed']:,} -> {tb['missed']:,}, viable {viable(ta):,} -> {viable(tb):,}"
          + (f", real holes {holes_a:,} -> {holes_b:,}" if holes_a is not None else
             ", real holes - (a census recorded before the column)" if any(
                 ha[k] is None or hb[k] is None for k in both) else ""))
    print()

    # The exact additive decomposition of the corpus move. Summed over crates this telescopes to
    # rate_b - rate_a, so the column is an attribution rather than a ranking: a crate's points are
    # what the corpus rate would have lost or gained had only that crate changed.
    vb = viable(tb) or 1
    rows = []
    for name in both:
        ka = ca[name]["caught"] + ca[name]["timeout"]
        kb = cb[name]["caught"] + cb[name]["timeout"]
        pts = 100.0 * ((kb - ka) - (rate_a / 100.0) * (viable(cb[name]) - viable(ca[name]))) / vb
        rows.append((name, ca[name], cb[name], pts, ha[name], hb[name]))
    rows.sort(key=lambda r: -abs(r[3]))

    print(f"{'crate':<44}{'viable':>16}{'killed':>18}{'survivors':>16}{'points':>9}{'real holes':>17}")
    for name, x, y, pts, xa, xb in rows:
        if abs(pts) < 0.005 and x["missed"] == y["missed"] and xa == xb:
            continue
        holes = f"{xa:>7} ->{xb:>5}" if xa is not None and xb is not None else \
            f"{'-':>7} ->{'-':>5}" if xa is None and xb is None else \
            f"{xa if xa is not None else '-':>7} ->{xb if xb is not None else '-':>5}"
        print(f"{name:<44}{viable(x):>7} ->{viable(y):>6}"
              f"{killed(x):>11.1f}% ->{killed(y):>6.1f}%"
              f"{x['missed']:>9} ->{y['missed']:>5}{pts:>9.2f}{holes:>17}")
    for name in only_a:
        c = ca[name]
        print(f"{name:<44}{viable(c):>7} ->{'-':>6}{killed(c):>11.1f}% ->{'-':>7}"
              f"{c['missed']:>9} ->{'-':>5}{'(gone)':>9}")
    for name in only_b:
        c = cb[name]
        print(f"{name:<44}{'-':>7} ->{viable(c):>6}{'-':>12} ->{killed(c):>6.1f}%"
              f"{'-':>10} ->{c['missed']:>5}{'(new)':>9}")
    print()
    print("`points` sums to the corpus move above; a crate moves it by changing its own rate or by "
          "growing against the corpus average. `real holes` reads from the census record's own "
          "column, so a census recorded before the column shows `-`.")
    return 0


def listing():
    record = load_record()
    if not record:
        print(f"mutation-census: {RECORD} is empty", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print(f"{'census':<12}{'run':<14}{'crates':>7}{'mutants':>9}{'viable':>8}{'survivors':>10}{'killed':>9}")
    for census in sorted(record):
        crates = record[census]
        run = next(iter(crates.values())).run
        t = total(row.counts for row in crates.values())
        print(f"{census:<12}{run:<14}{len(crates):>7}{sum(t[k] for k in KINDS):>9}"
              f"{viable(t):>8}{t['missed']:>10}{killed(t):>8.1f}%")
    return 0


def main(argv):
    if argv[:1] == ["--selftest"]:
        return selftest()
    if not argv:
        return listing()
    mode, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    if mode in ("-h", "--help"):
        return usage()
    if mode == "--list":
        return listing()
    if mode == "--renames":
        return renames()
    if mode == "--add-baseline":
        return add_baseline()
    if mode == "--check-baseline":
        return add_baseline(check=True)
    if mode == "--save-baseline" and len(rest) == 1:
        return save_baseline(rest[0])
    if mode == "--show" and len(rest) == 1:
        return show(rest[0])
    if mode == "--compare" and len(rest) == 2:
        return compare(rest[0], rest[1])
    if mode == "--add-run" and len(rest) == 1:
        return add_run(rest[0])
    if mode == "--add" and len(rest) >= 3:
        return add(rest[0], rest[1], counts_from_dirs(rest[2:]),
                   mutation_real_holes.real_holes_from_shards(rest[2:]))
    return usage()


def selftest():
    """Fixtures for the record's own column: the round-trip, a pre-column file, the report and the
    comparison, and a rename group whose blank member poisons the group's count. No git, no
    network, no census."""
    global RECORD, load_renames
    import contextlib
    saved = RECORD
    saved_renames = load_renames
    try:
        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
            RECORD = pathlib.Path(tmp) / "record.csv"
            load_renames = lambda: {"od": "d"}
            record = {
                "2026-10-26": {"c": Row("1", collections.Counter(caught=8, missed=2, timeout=0,
                                                                 unviable=1), "2"),
                               "d": Row("1", collections.Counter(caught=5, missed=1, timeout=0,
                                                                 unviable=0), "3"),
                               "od": Row("1", collections.Counter(caught=4, missed=0, timeout=0,
                                                                  unviable=0), "")},
                "2026-10-19": {"c": Row("0", collections.Counter(caught=9, missed=1, timeout=0,
                                                                 unviable=1), ""),
                               "d": Row("0", collections.Counter(caught=5, missed=1, timeout=0,
                                                                 unviable=0), "1")},
            }
            write_record(record)
            got = load_record()
            assert got["2026-10-26"]["c"].real_holes == "2", got
            assert got["2026-10-19"]["c"].real_holes == "", got
            assert got["2026-10-26"]["c"].counts["missed"] == 2, got
            # --add records the holes it is given, and a census without one records blank cells
            add("2026-11-02", "5", {"c": collections.Counter(caught=9, missed=1, timeout=0,
                                                             unviable=0)}, holes={"c": 0})
            add("2026-11-09", "6", {"c": collections.Counter(caught=10, missed=0, timeout=0,
                                                             unviable=0)})
            got = load_record()
            assert got["2026-11-02"]["c"].real_holes == "0", got
            assert got["2026-11-09"]["c"].real_holes == "", got
            out = io.StringIO()
            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out):
                show("2026-10-26")
                show("2026-11-09")
                compare("2026-10-19", "2026-10-26")
                compare("2026-11-02", "2026-10-26")
            text = out.getvalue()
            assert text.count("real holes") >= 4, text
            assert "80.0%           2" in text, text
            assert "recorded before real holes were measured" in text, text
            assert "real holes - (a census recorded before the column)" in text, text
            assert "- ->    2" in text, text
            assert "real holes 0 -> 2" in text, text
            # a rename group with a blank member carries None for the whole group, never a sum over
            # the members that have counts: `d` measured 1 then, and 3 plus an unmeasured `od` now
            assert "1 ->    -" in text, text
            # a record written before the column reads with blank cells everywhere
            RECORD.write_text("census,run,crate,tested,caught,missed,timeout,unviable\n"
                              "2026-10-12,9,x,10,9,1,0,0\n")
            got = load_record()
            assert got["2026-10-12"]["x"].real_holes == "", got
            out = io.StringIO()
            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out):
                show("2026-10-12")
            assert "recorded before real holes were measured" in out.getvalue()
    finally:
        RECORD = saved
        load_renames = saved_renames
    print("mutation-census: selftest ok")
    return 0


sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
